WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.088
This is an implementation guide.

2
00:00:02.088 --> 00:00:07.385
The Federal Modernization Workbook's Book
08 sets the program's design for DNS,

3
00:00:07.385 --> 00:00:11.623
DHCP and IP address management,
and the Research section's deep

4
00:00:11.623 --> 00:00:16.187
dive explains why Infoblox is one
of the strongest platforms for it.

5
00:00:16.187 --> 00:00:21.200
This guide shows how to build that
design on Infoblox NIOS, version 9.1,

6
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.213
and how to move off Microsoft DNS
and DHCP without breaking anything.

7
00:00:26.213 --> 00:00:29.424
It runs in four parts
after a short orientation.

8
00:00:29.424 --> 00:00:33.354
Part 1 builds the Grid: the
Grid Master, its candidate,

9
00:00:33.354 --> 00:00:38.013
members, time, licensing, and
virtual appliances in the clouds.

10
00:00:38.013 --> 00:00:42.514
Part 2 migrates from Microsoft:
managing the Windows servers first,

11
00:00:42.514 --> 00:00:47.900
importing zones, Active Directory zones
and secure updates, and taking over DHCP.

12
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:52.993
Part 3 signs and hardens: DNSSEC,
the hardware security module,

13
00:00:52.993 --> 00:00:57.088
the DISA STIG, FIPS mode
and administrator access.

14
00:00:57.088 --> 00:01:01.452
And Part 4 covers running it:
upgrades, backup and recovery,

15
00:01:01.452 --> 00:01:06.362
logging, automation, and the build
mapped onto Book 08's six phases.

16
00:01:06.362 --> 00:01:10.087
The ground rules are the same as
the other implementation guides.

17
00:01:10.087 --> 00:01:12.936
Every fact comes from a primary source;

18
00:01:12.936 --> 00:01:16.851
the new ones were opened on
October twenty-third, 2026.

19
00:01:16.851 --> 00:01:20.394
Sequencing recommendations
are labeled design guidance,

20
00:01:20.394 --> 00:01:22.925
and inferences are labeled as inferences.

21
00:01:22.925 --> 00:01:25.736
Sources: Research brief,

22
00:01:25.736 --> 00:01:33.185
Implementing Book 08's DDI design
on Infoblox NIOS (verified Oct.

23
00:01:33.185 --> 00:01:42.321
23, 2026), with the earlier verified Infoblox
briefs it reuses; Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x,

24
00:01:42.321 --> 00:01:46.959
NIOS upgrade and FIPS/CC documentation;

25
00:01:46.959 --> 00:01:54.350
DISA Infoblox NIOS 9.x STIG V1R1;
Book 08, Chapters 04, 06, 08 to 11.

26
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.625
What does this guide implement?

27
00:01:56.625 --> 00:02:00.410
The Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08's design,

28
00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:02.850
mapped onto the way Infoblox works.

29
00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:08.325
Book 08 wants governed authoritative
DNS and IP address management.

30
00:02:08.325 --> 00:02:12.071
On NIOS that's a Grid: one Grid
Master holds the database,

31
00:02:12.071 --> 00:02:15.900
and every member gets its copy
over an encrypted tunnel.

32
00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.100
Part 1 builds it.

33
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:22.738
Book 08 wants a coexistence period
with Microsoft DNS and DHCP.

34
00:02:22.738 --> 00:02:26.913
On NIOS, managing members
synchronize the Windows servers over

35
00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:30.851
Microsoft's remote procedure
call protocol, read-only first.

36
00:02:30.851 --> 00:02:35.899
Book 08 moves zones and scopes one at
a time; on NIOS that's zone import

37
00:02:35.899 --> 00:02:41.038
by transfer, and a DHCP takeover
done by changing server assignments.

38
00:02:41.038 --> 00:02:46.447
Book 08's default keeps the Active Directory
locator records on AD DNS until the

39
00:02:46.447 --> 00:02:50.709
last domain controller goes, and
this guide keeps that default,

40
00:02:50.709 --> 00:02:54.562
showing NIOS hosting the AD
zone only as the alternative.

41
00:02:54.562 --> 00:02:56.325
Part 2 covers all of that.

42
00:02:56.325 --> 00:02:59.838
Book 08 Chapter 06 requires DNSSEC.

43
00:02:59.838 --> 00:03:03.325
On NIOS, the Grid Master
signs as a hidden primary,

44
00:03:03.325 --> 00:03:07.337
with keys in its database or
a hardware security module.

45
00:03:07.337 --> 00:03:08.462
That's Part 3.

46
00:03:08.462 --> 00:03:12.765
And Chapter 11's evidence becomes
secure syslog to the SIEM,

47
00:03:12.765 --> 00:03:17.337
a copy of the audit log, backups
and the STIG checklist, in Part 4.

48
00:03:17.337 --> 00:03:20.237
Book 08's six phases still govern.

49
00:03:20.237 --> 00:03:22.864
This guide adds a Phase 0 of decisions,

50
00:03:22.864 --> 00:03:26.338
because some NIOS choices
can't be undone cheaply.

51
00:03:26.338 --> 00:03:31.735
Sources: Book 08, Chapters 04
(AD DNS departure), 06 (DNSSEC),

52
00:03:31.735 --> 00:03:37.134
08 (reference architecture),
10 (six phases, cutover loop),

53
00:03:37.134 --> 00:03:44.931
11 (operations and evidence); Infoblox
NIOS 9.1.x About Grids, Managing Microsoft

54
00:03:44.931 --> 00:03:53.210
Windows servers, Importing zone data, DNSSEC
on a Grid; research brief section 10.

55
00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:58.798
Before Phase 1, four decisions,
because each is expensive to reverse.

56
00:03:58.798 --> 00:04:00.673
First, FIPS mode.

57
00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:03.960
On NIOS you can set it only
from the command line,

58
00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.585
and only after a factory
reset, the reset all command.

59
00:04:07.585 --> 00:04:11.673
On a Grid you set it on the Grid Master,
and members take it when they join.

60
00:04:11.673 --> 00:04:14.372
Turning it off again also needs reset all.

61
00:04:14.372 --> 00:04:19.372
And enabling it disables every cloud
connection, including the Infoblox Portal.

62
00:04:19.372 --> 00:04:23.382
So a FIPS-mode Grid can't use
Universal DDI hybrid management,

63
00:04:23.382 --> 00:04:28.034
and by my inference it loses the
portal-managed Data Connector as well.

64
00:04:28.034 --> 00:04:30.513
Be precise about the claim, too:

65
00:04:30.513 --> 00:04:36.954
Infoblox says NIOS 9.0.7 and later is
supported in FIPS and Common Criteria mode,

66
00:04:36.954 --> 00:04:43.297
but that the FIPS 140-3 and Common
Criteria certifications are still pending.

67
00:04:43.297 --> 00:04:47.910
Running in FIPS mode isn't the same
as holding a validated module.

68
00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:50.372
Second, the Long Term Support train.

69
00:04:50.372 --> 00:04:53.761
One of the STIG's category one
rules measures you against the

70
00:04:53.761 --> 00:04:57.422
current approved long-term-support
version for federal systems.

71
00:04:57.422 --> 00:04:59.711
Third, the DNSSEC defaults.

72
00:04:59.711 --> 00:05:06.372
NIOS defaults to RSA with SHA-1,
and a 1024-bit zone-signing key.

73
00:05:06.372 --> 00:05:10.084
Change both before you sign
anything, because fixing it later

74
00:05:10.084 --> 00:05:13.723
means an algorithm rollover and
new DS records at the parent.

75
00:05:13.723 --> 00:05:16.385
And fourth, the hardware security module.

76
00:05:16.385 --> 00:05:21.022
The STIG wants the Grid Master hidden
from every zone's name server list,

77
00:05:21.022 --> 00:05:23.773
and HSM signing, or a
documented exception.

78
00:05:23.773 --> 00:05:32.222
Sources: Infoblox NIOS FIPS and CC
mode: Enabling FIPS or CC mode,

79
00:05:32.222 --> 00:05:41.972
and Guidelines for FIPS/CC mode; NIOS
9.1.x Configuring DNSSEC parameters;

80
00:05:41.972 --> 00:05:48.147
DISA Infoblox NIOS 9.x
STIG V1R1, rules V-285229,

81
00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:55.650
V-285269 and V-285308; research
brief sections 0.1 to 0.3.

82
00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.150
Part 1: build the Grid.

83
00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:02.100
We'll start with the Grid Master,
built as a high-availability pair,

84
00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:06.562
and its candidate, the appliance that
takes over if the Grid Master is lost.

85
00:06:06.562 --> 00:06:10.875
Then how members join, the ports
the Grid needs, time, and licenses,

86
00:06:10.875 --> 00:06:15.350
including one licensing trap that
can evict a member from the Grid.

87
00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:19.204
And finally the virtual
appliances, vNIOS, on VMware,

88
00:06:19.204 --> 00:06:24.313
Azure and AWS, where the government
regions have a documentation gap.

89
00:06:24.313 --> 00:06:30.131
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x Grid
setup, HA, joining members, ports,

90
00:06:30.131 --> 00:06:37.852
NTP and license documentation; vNIOS for
VMware, Azure and AWS installation guides;

91
00:06:37.852 --> 00:06:43.470
the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08, Chapter 08.

92
00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:45.458
The Grid Master.

93
00:06:45.458 --> 00:06:49.499
Infoblox's sequence is simple:
create the Grid Master first,

94
00:06:49.499 --> 00:06:54.283
then add members, and build the Grid
Master as a high-availability pair.

95
00:06:54.283 --> 00:06:58.015
Physically, cable each node's
LAN1 and HA ports to the switch,

96
00:06:58.015 --> 00:07:01.671
and put the nodes on separate
power circuits where you can.

97
00:07:01.671 --> 00:07:05.032
The pair uses VRRP, so
the switch ports matter.

98
00:07:05.032 --> 00:07:07.308
Spanning tree off, or Portfast.

99
00:07:07.308 --> 00:07:09.582
No EtherChannel or port channeling.

100
00:07:09.582 --> 00:07:11.121
IGMP snooping off.

101
00:07:11.121 --> 00:07:13.445
Trunking off unless you tag VLANs.

102
00:07:13.445 --> 00:07:15.345
Speed and duplex matched.

103
00:07:15.345 --> 00:07:18.488
And DHCP snooping disabled or trusted;

104
00:07:18.488 --> 00:07:23.007
Infoblox says it must be disabled
to run DHCP on the Grid.

105
00:07:23.007 --> 00:07:26.670
On node one, run the wizard
to configure a Grid Master.

106
00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.525
It enters the Grid
name, the shared secret,

107
00:07:29.525 --> 00:07:34.143
a virtual router ID that's unique
on the subnet, the virtual address,

108
00:07:34.143 --> 00:07:38.258
and each node's addresses, plus
the time zone and NTP servers.

109
00:07:38.258 --> 00:07:41.937
On node two, run Join Existing
Grid with the same name,

110
00:07:41.937 --> 00:07:44.419
the virtual address and the secret.

111
00:07:44.419 --> 00:07:47.695
After that, manage the pair
through the virtual address.

112
00:07:47.695 --> 00:07:52.368
Now the defaults, which you must
change: the login is admin and infoblox,

113
00:07:52.368 --> 00:07:56.558
the Grid name is Infoblox, and the
shared secret is the word test.

114
00:07:56.558 --> 00:07:59.844
Escrow the new secret; Infoblox
says if you forget it,

115
00:07:59.844 --> 00:08:01.983
you have to contact their support.

116
00:08:01.983 --> 00:08:06.809
Two more rules: no IPv6 high availability
on public clouds, and appliances

117
00:08:06.809 --> 00:08:11.794
with less than two hundred fifty gigabytes
of disk shouldn't be Grid Masters.

118
00:08:11.794 --> 00:08:15.921
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x
Creating a Grid Master;

119
00:08:15.921 --> 00:08:20.249
Setting up an HA Grid Master
(physical placement, LCD);

120
00:08:20.249 --> 00:08:26.690
Guidelines for switch ports in an HA
pair; Configure a Grid Master wizard; Join

121
00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:28.501
Existing Grid wizard;

122
00:08:28.501 --> 00:08:33.890
NIOS 9.x guide (disk size rule)
from the earlier platform brief.

123
00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:36.890
Next, the Grid Master Candidate.

124
00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:40.965
You designate it in the member's properties
with the Master Candidate option.

125
00:08:40.965 --> 00:08:43.140
It holds a full copy of the database,

126
00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:46.140
and it should be the same
model as the Grid Master.

127
00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:48.107
Infoblox gives you a promote test,

128
00:08:48.107 --> 00:08:52.252
which sends crafted packets from the
candidate to the members you pick.

129
00:08:52.252 --> 00:08:55.016
It runs for at most a
hundred twenty seconds,

130
00:08:55.016 --> 00:08:59.391
only one candidate can be tested at
a time, and never during an upgrade.

131
00:08:59.391 --> 00:09:04.702
One quirk: behind NAT in Azure, the test
fails but the real promotion works.

132
00:09:04.702 --> 00:09:05.915
Joining members.

133
00:09:05.915 --> 00:09:10.128
Define each member on the Grid Master
before you configure the appliance.

134
00:09:10.128 --> 00:09:14.181
Then join it with the Grid Master's
virtual address, the Grid name and

135
00:09:14.181 --> 00:09:18.378
the shared secret, through the wizard,
Grid Manager or the command line.

136
00:09:18.378 --> 00:09:23.766
Status icons show green for joined, yellow
for joining and red for not joined.

137
00:09:23.766 --> 00:09:26.903
A dual-mode Grid needs
a dual-mode candidate.

138
00:09:26.903 --> 00:09:27.877
The ports.

139
00:09:27.877 --> 00:09:32.612
Members need UDP 2114 for
key exchange and UDP 1194 for

140
00:09:32.612 --> 00:09:37.241
the Grid's VPN tunnel to the
Grid Master and candidate.

141
00:09:37.241 --> 00:09:40.577
HA pairs use VRRP and must share a subnet.

142
00:09:40.577 --> 00:09:47.927
DHCP failover uses TCP 647,
and 7911 for partner down.

143
00:09:47.927 --> 00:09:54.703
Managing Windows servers needs 135,
445 and the dynamic RPC range.

144
00:09:54.703 --> 00:09:57.890
And secure syslog uses 6514.

145
00:09:57.890 --> 00:10:00.028
But FIPS mode changes this.

146
00:10:00.028 --> 00:10:07.828
The Grid's OpenVPN runs over TCP 22, and
port 1023 carries HA passive to active.

147
00:10:07.828 --> 00:10:11.977
So, my inference, firewall
rules written only

148
00:10:11.977 --> 00:10:16.127
for 1194 and 2114 won't
be enough on a FIPS Grid.

149
00:10:16.127 --> 00:10:23.867
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x Designating
a Grid Master Candidate; GMC Promote Test;

150
00:10:23.867 --> 00:10:28.604
Joining appliances to the
Grid; Defining members;

151
00:10:28.604 --> 00:10:34.611
Source and destination ports for
services (version 2, dated Oct.

152
00:10:34.611 --> 00:10:41.780
6, 2026); NIOS FIPS/CC Enabling FIPS or
CC mode; vNIOS for Azure limitations.

153
00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:45.568
Two quiet failure modes,
time and licenses.

154
00:10:45.568 --> 00:10:46.642
Time first.

155
00:10:46.642 --> 00:10:50.161
Enable NTP and set the external
servers at the Grid level

156
00:10:50.161 --> 00:10:53.905
before you configure the Grid
Master and members as clients.

157
00:10:53.905 --> 00:10:58.494
The Grid Master then serves time to the
members inside the encrypted tunnel,

158
00:10:58.494 --> 00:11:03.155
and if it goes offline, the candidate
and members sync to the external servers.

159
00:11:03.155 --> 00:11:05.573
The STIG requires the external servers at

160
00:11:05.573 --> 00:11:08.130
the Grid level and
recommends at least three.

161
00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:11.678
A clock more than five minutes
off is stepped immediately;

162
00:11:11.678 --> 00:11:13.230
a smaller error is slewed.

163
00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:16.537
Time isn't cosmetic here:
Grid synchronization,

164
00:11:16.537 --> 00:11:20.693
Kerberos-signed updates, and
DHCP failover all depend on it.

165
00:11:20.693 --> 00:11:24.501
One gap to note for your control mapping:

166
00:11:24.501 --> 00:11:32.004
NIOS lists NTP authentication keys of MD5,
DES and SHA-1 only, with no SHA-2 option.

167
00:11:32.004 --> 00:11:33.030
Licenses.

168
00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:37.473
Member licenses bind to a hardware
ID, Grid-wide licenses bind to the

169
00:11:37.473 --> 00:11:42.155
Grid Master and replicate, and
subscriptions expire with the contract.

170
00:11:42.155 --> 00:11:44.330
The trap is the temporary license.

171
00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:48.224
It lasts sixty days and can't be
renewed, and when it expires,

172
00:11:48.224 --> 00:11:51.955
the member's services stop and
it's evicted from the Grid.

173
00:11:51.955 --> 00:11:54.643
So never let a production
member run on one.

174
00:11:54.643 --> 00:11:57.708
Install licenses on both
nodes of an HA pair,

175
00:11:57.708 --> 00:12:02.392
and expect the NIOS and some add-on
licenses to restart the product.

176
00:12:02.392 --> 00:12:05.217
If the Microsoft Management
license expires,

177
00:12:05.217 --> 00:12:08.117
synchronization to the
Windows servers stops.

178
00:12:08.117 --> 00:12:11.564
For VMware, get permanent
keys by submitting

179
00:12:11.564 --> 00:12:14.730
each VM's hardware ID,
and allow a day or two.

180
00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:22.859
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x Configuring
NTP (Grid and member), NTP clock behavior,

181
00:12:22.859 --> 00:12:31.238
NTP authentication keys; License types,
Expiring licenses, Installing licenses;

182
00:12:31.238 --> 00:12:36.240
vNIOS for VMware obtaining
permanent licenses;

183
00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:40.720
DISA Infoblox NIOS 9.x
STIG V1R1, V-285309.

184
00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:45.233
Virtual appliances, vNIOS,
in each environment.

185
00:12:45.233 --> 00:12:50.594
On VMware, the OVA comes from Infoblox's
support site, which needs a login.

186
00:12:50.594 --> 00:12:54.197
Infoblox says not to change
the VM's settings in vSphere,

187
00:12:54.197 --> 00:12:59.208
because that may violate the virtual
licensing terms and stop the VM joining.

188
00:12:59.208 --> 00:13:02.899
Mixed SR-IOV and regular
network cards aren't supported,

189
00:13:02.899 --> 00:13:04.745
and cloned VMs aren't either.

190
00:13:04.745 --> 00:13:08.246
On Azure, marketplace deployment
is supported on Azure

191
00:13:08.246 --> 00:13:11.595
public and Azure Government,
with managed disks only.

192
00:13:11.595 --> 00:13:17.795
High availability arrived in 9.0.4, and
needs a custom role and a managed identity.

193
00:13:17.795 --> 00:13:23.859
The limits to plan around: DHCP failover
doesn't work behind NAT, there's no LAN2,

194
00:13:23.859 --> 00:13:29.259
no IPv6 high availability, and both
HA nodes must be in the same region,

195
00:13:29.259 --> 00:13:32.670
resource group,
availability zone and size.

196
00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:39.395
On AWS, high availability also starts at
9.0.4, and needs the VPC and subnets,

197
00:13:39.395 --> 00:13:46.457
an HA IAM role, and a Grid DNS resolver
that can reach the IAM and EC2 endpoints.

198
00:13:46.457 --> 00:13:52.132
Same limits: no IPv6 HA, same
region, zone and instance type.

199
00:13:52.132 --> 00:13:53.121
Now the gap.

200
00:13:53.121 --> 00:13:56.770
The HA prerequisites list
only commercial endpoints.

201
00:13:56.770 --> 00:14:01.283
AWS lists the global and
China endpoints, not GovCloud.

202
00:14:01.283 --> 00:14:06.460
Azure lists management dot azure dot com
and login dot microsoftonline dot com,

203
00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:08.970
with no Azure Government equivalents.

204
00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:12.358
And failover time depends
on the cloud's own API.

205
00:14:12.358 --> 00:14:14.309
So ask Infoblox, in writing,

206
00:14:14.309 --> 00:14:18.833
before you rely on vNIOS failover
in either government cloud.

207
00:14:18.833 --> 00:14:21.483
One more constraint that shapes Part 2:

208
00:14:21.483 --> 00:14:24.632
vNIOS members can't
manage Microsoft servers.

209
00:14:24.632 --> 00:14:31.728
Sources: Infoblox vNIOS for VMware
installation guide (OVA, VM settings, SR-IOV);

210
00:14:31.728 --> 00:14:38.158
vNIOS for Azure (Marketplace support,
managed disks, HA prerequisites,

211
00:14:38.158 --> 00:14:44.255
custom role and managed identity,
limitations page version 19, Aug.

212
00:14:44.255 --> 00:14:49.355
31, 2026); vNIOS for AWS (HA
prerequisites and limitations);

213
00:14:49.355 --> 00:14:54.580
NIOS 9.1.x Guidelines for
managing Microsoft servers.

214
00:14:55.330 --> 00:14:57.780
Part 2: migrate from Microsoft.

215
00:14:57.780 --> 00:15:01.780
This is the longest part, because
it's where a mistake hurts users.

216
00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:06.267
We'll start with the method the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 08's

217
00:15:06.267 --> 00:15:08.033
coexistence phase relies on:

218
00:15:08.033 --> 00:15:12.005
Infoblox managing the Windows
servers before it takes them over.

219
00:15:12.005 --> 00:15:17.142
Then zone import, Active Directory zones
and Kerberos-signed dynamic updates,

220
00:15:17.142 --> 00:15:21.567
where a Microsoft change from July
2026 breaks the vendor's example.

221
00:15:21.567 --> 00:15:26.749
Then the DHCP takeover, which has
one destructive step to avoid,

222
00:15:26.749 --> 00:15:31.830
DHCP failover and IPv6, and
finally how each method rolls back.

223
00:15:31.830 --> 00:15:38.128
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x
Managing Microsoft Windows servers,

224
00:15:38.128 --> 00:15:44.307
Importing zone data, GSS-TSIG
and DHCP failover documentation;

225
00:15:44.307 --> 00:15:51.210
Microsoft Learn DNS, DHCP and Kerberos
RC4 pages; Book 08, Chapter 10.

226
00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:56.919
Method A, which the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08's coexistence

227
00:15:56.919 --> 00:16:01.398
phase leans on: manage the Windows
servers first, then take them over.

228
00:16:01.398 --> 00:16:06.662
Grid members manage Windows DNS
and DHCP servers without an agent,

229
00:16:06.662 --> 00:16:11.448
over Microsoft's RPC protocol,
in read-only or read-write mode.

230
00:16:11.448 --> 00:16:13.773
Edits sync in both directions.

231
00:16:13.773 --> 00:16:17.960
It needs the Microsoft Management
license on the managing member,

232
00:16:17.960 --> 00:16:21.310
and as we saw, that member
must be a physical appliance.

233
00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:24.925
Infoblox recommends dedicated
managing members close to

234
00:16:24.925 --> 00:16:28.172
the Windows servers, not the
Grid Master or candidate.

235
00:16:28.172 --> 00:16:33.449
The service account needs DnsAdmins
for DNS, DHCP Administrators for DHCP,

236
00:16:33.449 --> 00:16:38.810
and the right to start and stop the
services, rather than full administrator.

237
00:16:38.810 --> 00:16:43.993
What syncs: forward and reverse zones,
Active Directory-integrated zones,

238
00:16:43.993 --> 00:16:47.222
stub zones, delegations
and most record types.

239
00:16:47.222 --> 00:16:50.801
What doesn't: conditional
forwarders, trust anchors,

240
00:16:50.801 --> 00:16:54.297
cached and root zones, and
a few legacy record types.

241
00:16:54.297 --> 00:16:59.460
So you still need Book 08's PowerShell
export to recreate every forwarder.

242
00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:00.810
Three rules that bite.

243
00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:05.197
Each Windows server syncs into
one network view and one DNS view,

244
00:17:05.197 --> 00:17:08.260
so overlapping servers
need separate views.

245
00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:10.497
Schedule the first sync off-hours.

246
00:17:10.497 --> 00:17:14.825
And if an HA managing member fails
over mid-sync, that sync aborts,

247
00:17:14.825 --> 00:17:18.498
and Grid-side changes made
since the last sync are lost.

248
00:17:18.498 --> 00:17:23.395
The program's design guidance: in Phase
1, add the Windows servers read-only,

249
00:17:23.395 --> 00:17:27.810
and the synced views become a live
inventory alongside the export.

250
00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:32.726
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x
Managing Microsoft Windows servers;

251
00:17:32.726 --> 00:17:36.158
Guidelines for managing Microsoft servers;

252
00:17:36.158 --> 00:17:41.910
Requirements for the Microsoft server
account; Synchronized data; Book 08,

253
00:17:41.910 --> 00:17:47.550
Chapter 10 (Phase 1 illustrative
commands); research brief section 2.1.

254
00:17:48.300 --> 00:17:51.525
Method B: import zones by zone transfer.

255
00:17:51.525 --> 00:17:54.850
On NIOS, zone import is a zone transfer.

256
00:17:54.850 --> 00:17:59.392
The Windows server must be authoritative
and must allow transfers to the

257
00:17:59.392 --> 00:18:02.816
destination, which on a Grid
is the Grid Master's address,

258
00:18:02.816 --> 00:18:05.050
or its virtual address for an HA pair.

259
00:18:05.050 --> 00:18:10.014
On Windows, that's one PowerShell command
per zone, Set DNS Server Primary Zone,

260
00:18:10.014 --> 00:18:14.125
with the secure secondaries
setting pointed at the Grid Master.

261
00:18:14.125 --> 00:18:17.776
Then create the zone on NIOS
with the same name, import it,

262
00:18:17.776 --> 00:18:21.824
one zone with its subzones per
run, and validate record counts

263
00:18:21.824 --> 00:18:25.713
and answers before you change
any delegation or forwarder.

264
00:18:25.713 --> 00:18:30.262
That's the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08's cutover loop.

265
00:18:30.262 --> 00:18:34.189
What the import does: it
brings in address, alias,

266
00:18:34.189 --> 00:18:37.813
service, text, mail,
pointer and host records.

267
00:18:37.813 --> 00:18:43.652
It recreates the NS and SOA records for
the new primary rather than copying them,

268
00:18:43.652 --> 00:18:47.900
which matches Book 08's rule to
never migrate the zone apex.

269
00:18:47.900 --> 00:18:49.725
Subzones become delegations,

270
00:18:49.725 --> 00:18:53.375
and their records aren't imported,
so import them separately.

271
00:18:53.375 --> 00:18:56.587
If an import fails, the
zone is left disabled.

272
00:18:56.587 --> 00:19:01.413
And records with a blank name need
superuser rights or an explicit permission.

273
00:19:01.413 --> 00:19:06.226
For bulk data, Infoblox's Data Import
Wizard, from the support site,

274
00:19:06.226 --> 00:19:09.613
reads Microsoft, ISC,
VitalQIP and CSV formats,

275
00:19:09.613 --> 00:19:14.962
and the CSV Import Manager can verify
a file before it replaces anything.

276
00:19:14.962 --> 00:19:20.078
Infoblox warns that mass imports will
significantly affect member performance,

277
00:19:20.078 --> 00:19:23.688
resulting in service outage, so
run them in a change window.

278
00:19:23.688 --> 00:19:29.194
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x Importing
zone data; Data Import Wizard;

279
00:19:29.194 --> 00:19:32.999
CSV Import Manager; Mass
operations guidance;

280
00:19:32.999 --> 00:19:39.406
Microsoft Learn Set-DnsServerPrimaryZone
(Windows Server 2025 PowerShell)

281
00:19:39.406 --> 00:19:45.570
and Configure zone transfer settings;
Book 08, Chapter 10 (cutover loop).

282
00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:50.295
Active Directory zones and
Kerberos-signed dynamic updates.

283
00:19:50.295 --> 00:19:53.295
Start with a correction to
the vendor's documentation.

284
00:19:53.295 --> 00:19:58.512
Infoblox's NIOS 9.1 page for generating
the keytab, the key file NIOS uses

285
00:19:58.512 --> 00:20:03.995
for Kerberos-signed updates, shows the
ktpass command with RC4 encryption.

286
00:20:03.995 --> 00:20:09.425
Microsoft's July 2026 security update
moved Kerberos RC4 protections into

287
00:20:09.425 --> 00:20:13.562
enforcement mode on domain
controllers, with no rollback,

288
00:20:13.562 --> 00:20:19.595
and Windows Server 2025 domain controllers
don't issue RC4 ticket-granting tickets.

289
00:20:19.595 --> 00:20:23.432
So generate the keytab with AES 256.

290
00:20:23.432 --> 00:20:28.458
Note that ktpass also resets the account's
password and increments its key version.

291
00:20:28.458 --> 00:20:34.093
The Federal Modernization Workbook's Book
08's default is that the locator zones

292
00:20:34.093 --> 00:20:39.308
stay on AD DNS, reached by forwarding,
until the last domain controller goes.

293
00:20:39.308 --> 00:20:43.084
Under that default, NIOS
still needs AES keytabs,

294
00:20:43.084 --> 00:20:47.521
loaded in Phase 2, so that its
DHCP can send signed updates.

295
00:20:47.521 --> 00:20:52.428
One limit: an Infoblox DHCP server sends
signed updates for one domain only,

296
00:20:52.428 --> 00:20:55.595
though each member can
serve a different domain.

297
00:20:55.595 --> 00:20:57.307
Keys survive upgrades.

298
00:20:57.307 --> 00:21:01.294
The alternative is NIOS
hosting the AD zone itself.

299
00:21:01.294 --> 00:21:05.809
Allow transfers from the domain
controller, import the AES keytab,

300
00:21:05.809 --> 00:21:10.733
create the zone with the exact AD name,
import it, and allow signed updates.

301
00:21:10.733 --> 00:21:13.592
The wizard creates the underscore zones,

302
00:21:13.592 --> 00:21:16.871
such as underscore msdcs
and underscore sites.

303
00:21:16.871 --> 00:21:19.808
Then clear the option that
allows unsigned updates.

304
00:21:19.808 --> 00:21:24.850
But the Infoblox pages list
domain controllers up to 2016 on

305
00:21:24.850 --> 00:21:30.094
one page and 2022 on another, and
none names Windows Server 2025.

306
00:21:30.094 --> 00:21:32.520
Confirm support before you take this path.

307
00:21:32.520 --> 00:21:39.240
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x Generating
the keytab file (Windows Server

308
00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:44.339
2016/2019/2022); Configuring
DNS for Active Directory;

309
00:21:44.339 --> 00:21:51.059
Configure Active Directory wizard;
About GSS-TSIG; Kerberos behavior;

310
00:21:51.059 --> 00:21:58.938
GSS-TSIG and upgrades; Microsoft Learn
Detect and remediate RC4 usage in Kerberos;

311
00:21:58.938 --> 00:22:05.550
Windows message center (July 2026
enforcement); Book 08, Chapter 04.

312
00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:10.100
The DHCP takeover, which
has one destructive trap.

313
00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:15.471
Infoblox documents that when you remove
a managed Windows DHCP server from Grid

314
00:22:15.471 --> 00:22:18.998
management, Grid Manager
deletes all the DHCP ranges,

315
00:22:18.998 --> 00:22:22.125
leases and fixed addresses
associated with it.

316
00:22:22.125 --> 00:22:24.778
So the order is: take
the scopes over first,

317
00:22:24.778 --> 00:22:27.588
and remove the server
from management last.

318
00:22:27.588 --> 00:22:30.123
The takeover itself is five steps,

319
00:22:30.123 --> 00:22:34.562
from Infoblox's page on modifying
DHCP server assignments.

320
00:22:34.562 --> 00:22:38.049
Set every fixed address in
the scope to the server None.

321
00:22:38.049 --> 00:22:41.588
Set every range served by
the Windows server to None.

322
00:22:41.588 --> 00:22:46.237
In the network's member assignment, replace
the Windows server with a Grid member.

323
00:22:46.237 --> 00:22:48.275
Assign the ranges to that member.

324
00:22:48.275 --> 00:22:49.938
Then restart services.

325
00:22:49.938 --> 00:22:52.917
Infoblox says you don't need
to clear the old leases,

326
00:22:52.917 --> 00:22:56.463
because the member clears them
when the assignment changes.

327
00:22:56.463 --> 00:22:59.774
Now the client side, from
Microsoft's documentation.

328
00:22:59.774 --> 00:23:04.787
Windows clients renew by unicast to the
server that issued the lease at half the

329
00:23:04.787 --> 00:23:07.368
lease time, and rebind by broadcast to any

330
00:23:07.368 --> 00:23:09.950
server at eighty-seven and a half percent.

331
00:23:09.950 --> 00:23:12.706
So the old Windows scope
must stop answering,

332
00:23:12.706 --> 00:23:16.803
deactivated or with the service
stopped, or clients keep renewing

333
00:23:16.803 --> 00:23:20.975
with it and keep its options, including
the DNS servers in option six.

334
00:23:20.975 --> 00:23:23.367
What isn't documented is whether a client

335
00:23:23.367 --> 00:23:25.900
keeps the same address
across the takeover.

336
00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:28.081
My inference: pilot it on one scope,

337
00:23:28.081 --> 00:23:31.975
and lower lease times beforehand
to shorten the transition.

338
00:23:31.975 --> 00:23:36.676
Then follow the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08's last step,

339
00:23:36.676 --> 00:23:38.738
de-authorizing the old server.

340
00:23:38.738 --> 00:23:44.106
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x
Modifying DHCP server assignments;

341
00:23:44.106 --> 00:23:50.507
Removing a Microsoft server from Grid
management; Synchronized DHCP data;

342
00:23:50.507 --> 00:23:57.321
Microsoft Learn Troubleshoot problems on
the DHCP client (renewal at 50 and 87.5

343
00:23:57.321 --> 00:24:03.770
percent); Book 08, Chapter 10 (Phase 3
DHCP scopes, Phase 5 de-authorization).

344
00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:07.708
DHCP failover, and IPv6.

345
00:24:07.708 --> 00:24:10.970
The rules, from Infoblox's failover page.

346
00:24:10.970 --> 00:24:13.240
Peers are two NIOS servers,

347
00:24:13.240 --> 00:24:18.295
or NIOS and an ISC-compliant
server; at least one must be NIOS.

348
00:24:18.295 --> 00:24:21.108
A mixed pair of Windows
and NIOS isn't offered,

349
00:24:21.108 --> 00:24:25.137
and that's why the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 08 says

350
00:24:25.137 --> 00:24:29.395
to move both partners of a Windows
failover relationship together.

351
00:24:29.395 --> 00:24:32.433
Peers talk on TCP 647 by default,

352
00:24:32.433 --> 00:24:39.445
or 519 on Grids upgraded from older
versions, and on 7911 for partner down.

353
00:24:39.445 --> 00:24:44.007
The defaults are a fifty-fifty load balance
and a maximum client lead time of one

354
00:24:44.007 --> 00:24:48.707
hour, and Infoblox says not to change them
unless you understand the consequences.

355
00:24:48.707 --> 00:24:52.548
Each pair of servers can be
in only one association,

356
00:24:52.548 --> 00:24:55.657
covering IPv4 ranges in one network view.

357
00:24:55.657 --> 00:24:59.071
And configure everything
before you enable DHCP.

358
00:24:59.071 --> 00:25:04.295
One correction to a common assumption:
the Grid Master plays no part in failover.

359
00:25:04.295 --> 00:25:05.845
The peers talk directly.

360
00:25:05.845 --> 00:25:09.047
On Azure, failover
doesn't work behind NAT,

361
00:25:09.047 --> 00:25:14.320
and Universal DDI's portal can't
manage NIOS failover associations.

362
00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:16.207
Then IPv6.

363
00:25:16.207 --> 00:25:19.532
An IPv6 range is served
by a single Grid member;

364
00:25:19.532 --> 00:25:22.332
there's no failover association for it.

365
00:25:22.332 --> 00:25:27.397
So redundancy comes from making that
member an HA pair, served through its

366
00:25:27.397 --> 00:25:32.795
virtual address, or, by my inference, from
non-overlapping ranges on two members.

367
00:25:32.795 --> 00:25:37.945
That isn't a regression: Microsoft's
DHCPv6 has no failover either.

368
00:25:37.945 --> 00:25:45.395
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x Configuring
DHCP failover associations (rules, ports,

369
00:25:45.395 --> 00:25:52.066
load balance, MCLT); IPv6 range wizard;
About bloxHA; DHCPv6 lease affinity;

370
00:25:52.066 --> 00:25:57.959
vNIOS for Azure limitations;
earlier platform brief (Universal

371
00:25:57.959 --> 00:26:03.760
DDI hybrid limits); Microsoft
Learn DHCP failover overview.

372
00:26:04.510 --> 00:26:06.322
Rollback, method by method.

373
00:26:06.322 --> 00:26:09.179
If you're managing the
Windows servers read-only,

374
00:26:09.179 --> 00:26:13.248
rollback is simply to stop managing
them; nothing on Windows changed.

375
00:26:13.248 --> 00:26:18.503
In read-write mode, be careful: changing
the managing member, or switching to

376
00:26:18.503 --> 00:26:23.759
read-only, reverts any Grid Manager
edits made since the last sync, silently.

377
00:26:23.759 --> 00:26:29.108
For zone import, the Windows zone stays
authoritative until you change delegation or

378
00:26:29.108 --> 00:26:33.928
forwarders, so the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08's cutover loop is

379
00:26:33.928 --> 00:26:38.598
the real rollback point, and NIOS can
also restore zone data after an import.

380
00:26:38.598 --> 00:26:43.260
For the DHCP takeover, I found
no Infoblox rollback procedure.

381
00:26:43.260 --> 00:26:48.058
My inference, mirroring the forward
steps: reverse the server assignments,

382
00:26:48.058 --> 00:26:51.385
reactivate the Windows scope,
and let clients renew.

383
00:26:51.385 --> 00:26:54.203
For configuration mistakes on NIOS itself,

384
00:26:54.203 --> 00:26:58.273
there's the database snapshot,
which you take from Grid Manager.

385
00:26:58.273 --> 00:27:03.097
Rolling back to it is faster than a
restore, but it covers only the database.

386
00:27:03.097 --> 00:27:08.561
There's only one, and a candidate promotion,
an upgrade or a restore deletes it.

387
00:27:08.561 --> 00:27:12.660
Don't use it to undo migration data
that clients already depend on.

388
00:27:12.660 --> 00:27:14.913
And Book 08's rule holds throughout:

389
00:27:14.913 --> 00:27:20.117
each phase ends with a rollback point and
a resolution-path consistency test, not

390
00:27:20.117 --> 00:27:25.322
a calendar date, and the Windows exports
are archived before anything is removed.

391
00:27:25.322 --> 00:27:30.572
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x
Microsoft server management modes;

392
00:27:30.572 --> 00:27:37.337
Importing zone data and Restoring zone
data; Modifying DHCP server assignments;

393
00:27:37.337 --> 00:27:41.173
Database snapshots;
earlier divisions brief

394
00:27:41.173 --> 00:27:45.370
(read/write revert behavior);
