395
00:27:46.120 --> 00:27:48.558
Part 3: sign and harden.

396
00:27:48.558 --> 00:27:52.104
First DNSSEC on NIOS: how
signing works on a Grid,

397
00:27:52.104 --> 00:27:57.107
the parameters to set before you
sign, and how to publish DS records.

398
00:27:57.107 --> 00:27:59.169
Then the hardware security module.

399
00:27:59.169 --> 00:28:02.245
Then what the DISA STIG
for NIOS 9 actually checks,

400
00:28:02.245 --> 00:28:04.570
which is narrower than people assume.

401
00:28:04.570 --> 00:28:09.038
Then the FIPS mode procedure, and
finally hardening administrator access,

402
00:28:09.038 --> 00:28:12.208
including two upgrade gates
that can lock people out.

403
00:28:12.208 --> 00:28:17.953
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x
DNSSEC and HSM documentation;

404
00:28:17.953 --> 00:28:24.798
NIOS FIPS and CC mode documentation;
DISA Infoblox NIOS 9.x STIG V1R1;

405
00:28:24.798 --> 00:28:30.910
the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08, Chapter 06.

406
00:28:31.660 --> 00:28:33.310
DNSSEC on NIOS.

407
00:28:33.310 --> 00:28:37.759
On a Grid, only the Grid Master
signs, acting as a hidden primary,

408
00:28:37.759 --> 00:28:41.510
and signing a zone makes it
the zone's primary server.

409
00:28:41.510 --> 00:28:44.843
You set the parameters in
the Grid's DNS properties,

410
00:28:44.843 --> 00:28:48.572
in advanced mode, and individual
zones can override them.

411
00:28:48.572 --> 00:28:52.710
Set them before you sign anything,
because the defaults fail the STIG.

412
00:28:52.710 --> 00:28:57.810
The default algorithm is RSA
with SHA-1, for both keys.

413
00:28:57.810 --> 00:29:06.572
Change it to RSA with SHA-256,
or ECDSA with P-256 or P-384.

414
00:29:06.572 --> 00:29:11.837
The default zone-signing key is 1024
bits; raise it to 2048 for RSA,

415
00:29:11.837 --> 00:29:17.997
and keep the key-signing key at least as
large, which is Infoblox's own advice.

416
00:29:17.997 --> 00:29:23.396
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 08 Chapter 06, citing NIST,

417
00:29:23.396 --> 00:29:28.510
says RSA keys of at least 2048
bits, with elliptic curve preferred.

418
00:29:28.510 --> 00:29:30.085
The other defaults are fine.

419
00:29:30.085 --> 00:29:35.135
Signature validity is four days, inside
the STIG's two-to-seven-day window.

420
00:29:35.135 --> 00:29:40.860
Denial of existence is NSEC3, which
the STIG requires for external zones.

421
00:29:40.860 --> 00:29:44.952
Zone-signing keys roll every
thirty days by pre-publication,

422
00:29:44.952 --> 00:29:46.835
which Infoblox recommends.

423
00:29:46.835 --> 00:29:49.674
Key-signing keys roll
yearly, automatically,

424
00:29:49.674 --> 00:29:54.048
with notifications when the parent's
DS record needs a manual update.

425
00:29:54.048 --> 00:29:59.433
One decision to document: Book 08
records NIST's preference for NSEC,

426
00:29:59.433 --> 00:30:02.798
while the STIG requires NSEC3 externally.

427
00:30:02.798 --> 00:30:04.711
Decide it for internal zones.

428
00:30:04.711 --> 00:30:09.519
And if you ever change a signed zone's
algorithm: add the new one, wait longer

429
00:30:09.519 --> 00:30:14.722
than the record lifetimes, update the parent's
DS, and only then remove the old one.

430
00:30:14.722 --> 00:30:24.427
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x Configuring
DNSSEC parameters (defaults and

431
00:30:24.427 --> 00:30:34.941
recommendations); Signing a zone; ZSK
rollover; KSK rollover and notifications;

432
00:30:34.941 --> 00:30:43.999
Changing algorithms; DISA Infoblox
NIOS 9.x STIG V1R1 rules V-285246,

433
00:30:43.999 --> 00:30:52.770
V-285265, V-285274 and V-285279; Book
08, Chapter 06 (NIST SP 800-81r3).

434
00:30:53.520 --> 00:30:57.257
The hardware security module,
and publishing DS records.

435
00:30:57.257 --> 00:31:00.085
The HSM is configured by a superuser,

436
00:31:00.085 --> 00:31:04.420
one HSM group at a time, and
deleting a group is permanent.

437
00:31:04.420 --> 00:31:12.795
NIOS supports Thales Luna, on TCP
1792, and Entrust nShield, on TCP 9004.

438
00:31:12.795 --> 00:31:16.053
Register both the Grid Master
and the candidate with it,

439
00:31:16.053 --> 00:31:19.595
because the candidate becomes
the signer after a promotion.

440
00:31:19.595 --> 00:31:22.753
For Luna, generate client
certificates for both,

441
00:31:22.753 --> 00:31:27.570
give HA nodes distinct names, and add
a route for the Grid's virtual address.

442
00:31:27.570 --> 00:31:30.333
For nShield, enroll both addresses.

443
00:31:30.333 --> 00:31:34.107
Then enable HSM signing,
which is a Grid-level setting.

444
00:31:34.107 --> 00:31:38.520
Signing moves to the HSM at the
next key rollover, not immediately.

445
00:31:38.520 --> 00:31:43.470
Key lifecycle lives on the HSM, and
NIOS never deletes keys from it.

446
00:31:43.470 --> 00:31:47.632
The Grid sends SNMP traps on
signing success or failure.

447
00:31:47.632 --> 00:31:49.508
Publishing DS records.

448
00:31:49.508 --> 00:31:55.370
If the parent zone is in the same Grid,
NIOS inserts the DS records automatically.

449
00:31:55.370 --> 00:32:01.508
Otherwise, export the trust anchors,
as DNSKEY, DS, or BIND format.

450
00:32:01.508 --> 00:32:07.419
The exported DS file carries
both SHA-1 and SHA-256 digests;

451
00:32:07.419 --> 00:32:13.332
my inference is to submit only
the SHA-256 one to the parent.

452
00:32:13.332 --> 00:32:17.869
And the Check KSK Rollover Due
tool lists keys due within a week.

453
00:32:17.869 --> 00:32:20.533
For zones already signed on Windows,

454
00:32:20.533 --> 00:32:25.771
follow the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08 Chapter 06's order:

455
00:32:25.771 --> 00:32:29.499
finish any rollover, sign on
NIOS, publish the new DS,

456
00:32:29.499 --> 00:32:32.695
validate, and only then
stop the old signing.

457
00:32:32.695 --> 00:32:36.795
NIOS doesn't sync Windows trust
anchors, so recreate them.

458
00:32:36.795 --> 00:32:43.646
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x HSM groups
overview, Thales Luna configuration,

459
00:32:43.646 --> 00:32:50.939
Entrust nShield configuration, Enabling
HSM signing; Exporting trust anchors;

460
00:32:50.939 --> 00:32:57.830
Check KSK rollover due; Source and
destination ports; Book 08, Chapter 06.

461
00:32:58.580 --> 00:33:01.843
What the DISA STIG for
NIOS 9 actually checks.

462
00:33:01.843 --> 00:33:05.947
Version one, release one,
accepted in August 2026,

463
00:33:05.947 --> 00:33:11.592
has forty-five rules: five in category
one and forty in category two.

464
00:33:11.592 --> 00:33:15.879
Its scope is only the DNS
capabilities of the appliance.

465
00:33:15.879 --> 00:33:21.855
DHCP and IPAM hardening aren't in it, so
those rely on the program's own baseline.

466
00:33:21.855 --> 00:33:25.468
It was written for the Department
of War, and the overview says

467
00:33:25.468 --> 00:33:29.293
other agencies are free to use it,
though some settings may not fit.

468
00:33:29.293 --> 00:33:31.367
The five category one rules.

469
00:33:31.367 --> 00:33:36.040
The Grid Master is stealth, hidden,
in every zone and name server group,

470
00:33:36.040 --> 00:33:38.992
which isn't a finding if
the keys are on an HSM.

471
00:33:38.992 --> 00:33:41.517
DNSSEC is enabled and validating.

472
00:33:41.517 --> 00:33:44.541
NIOS runs the current
approved long-term-support

473
00:33:44.541 --> 00:33:47.492
version for federal
systems, under maintenance.

474
00:33:47.492 --> 00:33:52.030
Cryptography is FIPS-validated,
so no RSA with SHA-1,

475
00:33:52.030 --> 00:33:55.380
which doesn't apply on a FIPS-mode Grid.

476
00:33:55.380 --> 00:33:57.467
And no DSA for DNSSEC.

477
00:33:57.467 --> 00:34:00.718
The forty category two
rules group into areas.

478
00:34:00.718 --> 00:34:03.643
Zone transfers only to named secondaries,

479
00:34:03.643 --> 00:34:08.406
and dynamic updates restricted by
Kerberos signing or access lists.

480
00:34:08.406 --> 00:34:11.070
Internal and external roles kept apart,

481
00:34:11.070 --> 00:34:14.705
with no recursion on external
authoritative servers.

482
00:34:14.705 --> 00:34:19.616
Administrator access through a
multi-factor policy with a remote service,

483
00:34:19.616 --> 00:34:22.230
a password policy, and only approved CAs.

484
00:34:22.230 --> 00:34:26.418
External syslog plus a copy
of the audit log to syslog.

485
00:34:26.418 --> 00:34:32.730
And resilience: a documented primary
promotion procedure, HSM signing, and NTP.

486
00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:34.975
One practical point from the overview:

487
00:34:34.975 --> 00:34:38.693
check these settings at the zone
level, not only at the Grid level.

488
00:34:38.693 --> 00:34:48.371
Sources: DISA Infoblox NIOS 9.x STIG
V1R1: XCCDF benchmark (July 28,

489
00:34:48.371 --> 00:34:54.945
2026) and Overview
document, downloaded Oct.

490
00:34:54.945 --> 00:34:58.232
22, 2026 and re-read Oct.

491
00:34:58.232 --> 00:35:05.354
23, 2026; rules V-285229,
V-285259, V-285269, V-285274,

492
00:35:05.354 --> 00:35:14.849
V-285279 and the CAT II rules cited
in research brief section 5.1;

493
00:35:14.849 --> 00:35:22.520
earlier federal brief
(Infoblox 8.x DNS STIG V1R4).

494
00:35:23.270 --> 00:35:26.607
FIPS mode: the procedure,
and what it changes.

495
00:35:26.607 --> 00:35:28.495
The procedure has five steps.

496
00:35:28.495 --> 00:35:31.120
Run reset all, the factory reset.

497
00:35:31.120 --> 00:35:33.508
Configure the network and licenses.

498
00:35:33.508 --> 00:35:37.382
Change the Grid's shared
secret, which in FIPS or Common

499
00:35:37.382 --> 00:35:41.257
Criteria mode must be twenty
to sixty-four characters.

500
00:35:41.257 --> 00:35:44.208
Run set fips mode, or set cc mode.

501
00:35:44.208 --> 00:35:47.067
The appliance reboots and runs self-tests,

502
00:35:47.067 --> 00:35:50.171
and if a self-test fails,
it loops on the console.

503
00:35:50.171 --> 00:35:54.495
On an HA pair, set the mode on each
node before you form the pair.

504
00:35:54.495 --> 00:35:58.683
Join any reporting or other special
members before you enable it.

505
00:35:58.683 --> 00:36:03.342
And after you upgrade a FIPS Grid,
run the distributed Grid communication

506
00:36:03.342 --> 00:36:08.232
mode command to make Grid traffic compliant;
a banner reminds you until you do.

507
00:36:08.232 --> 00:36:09.308
What changes.

508
00:36:09.308 --> 00:36:16.029
Only TLS 1.2 and 1.3 stay
enabled, and Infoblox flags DES,

509
00:36:16.029 --> 00:36:23.057
DSA, RSA with SHA-1 and MD5 as
not compliant with FIPS 140-3.

510
00:36:23.057 --> 00:36:25.707
Portal and cloud connections are disabled,

511
00:36:25.707 --> 00:36:29.094
and they aren't restored if
FIPS mode is later turned off.

512
00:36:29.094 --> 00:36:32.832
Threat rulesets and module sets
have to be uploaded by hand.

513
00:36:32.832 --> 00:36:36.382
In Common Criteria mode the
command line is console-only.

514
00:36:36.382 --> 00:36:40.007
The local admin password must
be changed on first login.

515
00:36:40.007 --> 00:36:46.445
On the STIG side, the rule against RSA with
SHA-1 doesn't apply to a FIPS-mode Grid.

516
00:36:46.445 --> 00:36:50.520
But remember slide three: the certificates
themselves are still pending.

517
00:36:50.520 --> 00:36:59.346
Sources: Infoblox NIOS FIPS and CC mode:
Enabling FIPS or CC mode (procedure, HA,

518
00:36:59.346 --> 00:37:08.732
shared secret, distributed Grid communication
mode); Guidelines for FIPS/CC mode

519
00:37:08.732 --> 00:37:12.935
(algorithms, TLS, portal, rulesets, CLI);

520
00:37:12.935 --> 00:37:17.930
DISA Infoblox NIOS 9.x
STIG V1R1, V-285274.

521
00:37:18.680 --> 00:37:21.693
Administrator access,
and two upgrade gates.

522
00:37:21.693 --> 00:37:25.295
Administrators sign in
with PIV through X.509

523
00:37:25.295 --> 00:37:29.467
client-certificate
authentication, checked by OCSP.

524
00:37:29.467 --> 00:37:33.952
The administrator's name must match
the certificate's common name,

525
00:37:33.952 --> 00:37:38.518
or NIOS can match on the UPN or email
in the subject alternative name.

526
00:37:38.518 --> 00:37:41.644
SAML works too, with
Entra and group mapping,

527
00:37:41.644 --> 00:37:44.517
but SAML-only users have no command line.

528
00:37:44.517 --> 00:37:47.399
Put the GUI and API on
the management port;

529
00:37:47.399 --> 00:37:51.467
serving them on both management
and LAN1 is off by default.

530
00:37:51.467 --> 00:37:56.655
Use the set security command to restrict
which source addresses can reach the GUI.

531
00:37:56.655 --> 00:38:01.080
Add the notice and consent banner, which
can be up to ten thousand characters.

532
00:38:01.080 --> 00:38:04.805
And keep the local admin as
break-glass for directory outages.

533
00:38:04.805 --> 00:38:09.243
Now two gates in the 9.1.0
upgrade that can lock people out.

534
00:38:09.243 --> 00:38:13.320
The test upgrade fails on administrator
usernames that don't comply,

535
00:38:13.320 --> 00:38:17.182
for example ones that don't start
with a letter or that mix scripts,

536
00:38:17.182 --> 00:38:20.330
and non-compliant users can't
log in after the upgrade.

537
00:38:20.330 --> 00:38:23.868
Check first with the show
non-compliant usernames command.

538
00:38:23.868 --> 00:38:29.242
And TLS 1.0 and 1.1 must be
turned off before the upgrade.

539
00:38:29.242 --> 00:38:34.324
For the STIG, the evidence is an authentication
policy that includes at least one

540
00:38:34.324 --> 00:38:37.509
remote service, with the
two-factor banner showing,

541
00:38:37.509 --> 00:38:41.529
a password policy, and only approved
CAs in the certificate list.

542
00:38:41.529 --> 00:38:44.439
The STIG names the
Department of War's PKI;

543
00:38:44.439 --> 00:38:49.743
my inference is that a civilian agency
reads that as its own approved CA set.

544
00:38:49.743 --> 00:38:59.119
Sources: Earlier divisions brief (CAC/PIV
and SAML authentication on NIOS);

545
00:38:59.119 --> 00:39:06.529
Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x set security
command; Using the MGMT port;

546
