and restore, Grid Master Candidate,

557
00:39:56.978 --> 00:40:02.607
logging and Data Connector documentation;
terraform-provider-nios and

558
00:40:02.607 --> 00:40:08.890
infoblox-ansible repositories; CISA KEV
catalog; Book 08, Chapters 10 and 11.

559
00:40:09.640 --> 00:40:10.552
Upgrades.

560
00:40:10.552 --> 00:40:13.881
Infoblox's process has
five steps: download,

561
00:40:13.881 --> 00:40:18.040
upload to the Grid Master,
distribute, test, and upgrade.

562
00:40:18.040 --> 00:40:21.578
Read the release notes first;
they list the addressed CVEs,

563
00:40:21.578 --> 00:40:23.790
and they're behind the support login.

564
00:40:23.790 --> 00:40:26.627
Upload goes to the
active Grid Master node.

565
00:40:26.627 --> 00:40:29.928
If it fails over before
distribution, upload again.

566
00:40:29.928 --> 00:40:32.602
Distribution is the point of no return.

567
00:40:32.602 --> 00:40:35.730
It loads the new software
into the alternate partition,

568
00:40:35.730 --> 00:40:39.402
which removes the ability to revert
to the version you had before.

569
00:40:39.402 --> 00:40:43.952
And once it starts, you can't
add, remove or regroup members.

570
00:40:43.952 --> 00:40:48.102
The test upgrade is
mandatory from 9.1.0 onward.

571
00:40:48.102 --> 00:40:49.646
It doesn't affect services,

572
00:40:49.646 --> 00:40:53.153
and it reports data translation
errors before they hurt you.

573
00:40:53.153 --> 00:40:56.014
Then the upgrade itself
runs by upgrade group.

574
00:40:56.014 --> 00:40:57.459
During a full upgrade,

575
00:40:57.459 --> 00:41:01.790
scheduled and approval tasks are
deleted, so recreate them after.

576
00:41:01.790 --> 00:41:04.993
Don't change member
DNS or DHCP properties,

577
00:41:04.993 --> 00:41:08.928
restart members not yet
upgraded, or rename members.

578
00:41:08.928 --> 00:41:12.624
HA pairs fail over, and if
DNSSEC operations are in flight,

579
00:41:12.624 --> 00:41:15.927
upgrade the candidate right
after the Grid Master.

580
00:41:15.927 --> 00:41:20.303
Infoblox expects you to finish within
a couple of days, and warns at nine.

581
00:41:20.303 --> 00:41:24.352
Reverting one member is possible
only while the upgrade is paused,

582
00:41:24.352 --> 00:41:27.665
within its revert window,
twenty-four hours by default.

583
00:41:27.665 --> 00:41:30.912
Reverting the whole Grid
restarts it at once and

584
00:41:30.912 --> 00:41:34.077
loses every change since,
including new leases.

585
00:41:34.077 --> 00:41:40.031
Long-term support: 9.0.6 in April 2025
started a twenty-four-month train,

586
00:41:40.031 --> 00:41:44.627
so by my inference its window
runs to about April 2027.

587
00:41:44.627 --> 00:41:47.977
Confirm the approved federal
build on the support portal.

588
00:41:47.977 --> 00:41:54.146
Sources: Infoblox NIOS upgrade guide:
Upgrading NIOS (five steps), Uploading,

589
00:41:54.146 --> 00:41:59.618
Distributing, Test upgrade, Full
and lite upgrades, Upgrade groups,

590
00:41:59.618 --> 00:42:04.394
During the upgrade, Reverting
a member, Reverting the Grid;

591
00:42:04.394 --> 00:42:10.420
Upgrade guidelines for NIOS 9.1.0;
earlier platform brief (LTS program).

592
00:42:11.170 --> 00:42:11.908
Patching.

593
00:42:11.908 --> 00:42:14.579
Infoblox's hotfix
procedure: back up first,

594
00:42:14.579 --> 00:42:19.160
then in Grid Manager apply the hotfix
to the Grid Master and every member,

595
00:42:19.160 --> 00:42:21.985
upload it, and use the
show action to activate

596
00:42:21.985 --> 00:42:25.345
hotfix command to see whether
a member needs a reboot.

597
00:42:25.345 --> 00:42:26.458
Two catches.

598
00:42:26.458 --> 00:42:30.408
Members that join the Grid after a
hotfix must have it applied again.

599
00:42:30.408 --> 00:42:34.046
And a custom hotfix has to go
through Infoblox Support first.

600
00:42:34.046 --> 00:42:35.786
On a Common Criteria Grid,

601
00:42:35.786 --> 00:42:39.683
upload the dot bin two upgrade
image, not the plain dot bin.

602
00:42:39.683 --> 00:42:43.858
And in 9.1.0, if the Grid has
Threat Insight or flex licenses,

603
00:42:43.858 --> 00:42:48.744
use the image that includes Threat
Insight, or distribution fails.

604
00:42:48.744 --> 00:42:55.457
For known exploited vulnerabilities,
I checked CISA's catalog,

605
00:42:55.457 --> 00:42:59.257
version 2026.10.21, with 1,754 entries.

606
00:42:59.257 --> 00:43:01.458
It lists no Infoblox product.

607
00:43:01.458 --> 00:43:07.201
It does list an old ISC BIND
vulnerability, CVE-2015-5477,

608
00:43:07.201 --> 00:43:13.820
added on October eighth, 2026,
with a short remediation deadline.

609
00:43:13.820 --> 00:43:19.795
Because NIOS 9.1 ships BIND 9.20, the
program's patch watch covers ISC's BIND

610
00:43:19.795 --> 00:43:25.870
advisories and the catalog's BIND
entries, not only Infoblox's own notices.

611
00:43:25.870 --> 00:43:29.770
Map each against the release
notes' list of addressed CVEs.

612
00:43:29.770 --> 00:43:36.438
Sources: Infoblox NIOS upgrade guide:
Applying consolidated hotfixes;

613
00:43:36.438 --> 00:43:40.733
NIOS 9.1.x uploading upgrade
images (bin2 for CC);

614
00:43:40.733 --> 00:43:45.819
Upgrade guidelines for NIOS
9.1.0 (Threat Insight image);

615
00:43:45.819 --> 00:43:51.357
CISA Known Exploited
Vulnerabilities catalog JSON feed,

616
00:43:51.357 --> 00:43:57.260
catalog version 2026.10.21;
earlier platform brief (BIND 9.20).

617
00:43:58.010 --> 00:44:01.597
Backup, restore, and
recovering the Grid Master.

618
00:44:01.597 --> 00:44:02.773
Backup first.

619
00:44:02.773 --> 00:44:06.147
Automatic backup is off by
default, so schedule it.

620
00:44:06.147 --> 00:44:09.239
Send it by SCP, over SSH version two,

621
00:44:09.239 --> 00:44:13.435
not FTP, which sends
credentials in clear text.

622
00:44:13.435 --> 00:44:18.290
One detail: SCP uses LAN1 even when
the management port is enabled,

623
00:44:18.290 --> 00:44:20.898
so the firewall path has to allow it.

624
00:44:20.898 --> 00:44:26.137
Back up before every upgrade, restore or
revert, and again after changing the Grid's

625
00:44:26.137 --> 00:44:31.298
IP mode, because restoring an older backup
afterward can stop members rejoining.

626
00:44:31.298 --> 00:44:32.209
Restore.

627
00:44:32.209 --> 00:44:37.588
SCP isn't listed as a restore source;
the choices are your computer,

628
00:44:37.588 --> 00:44:41.073
TFTP, FTP or the Grid
Master's local backups.

629
00:44:41.073 --> 00:44:44.699
My inference is to copy SCP
backups back to the management

630
00:44:44.699 --> 00:44:47.872
system and restore from
there, and to rehearse that.

631
00:44:47.872 --> 00:44:52.136
You can restore to the same version,
or to a later one on a supported path.

632
00:44:52.136 --> 00:44:55.133
A restore overwrites
everything and reboots,

633
00:44:55.133 --> 00:45:00.498
and a backup taken in Common Criteria mode
restores only to compliant appliances.

634
00:45:00.498 --> 00:45:04.785
Recovering from the loss of the Grid
Master means promoting the candidate.

635
00:45:04.785 --> 00:45:11.235
Confirm UDP 1194 and 2114 are open
from the candidate to every member.

636
00:45:11.235 --> 00:45:15.760
On the candidate's console or
SSH, run set promote master.

637
00:45:15.760 --> 00:45:21.437
Choose to notify members sequentially, which
Infoblox says can minimize DNS outages;

638
00:45:21.437 --> 00:45:24.635
the default spacing is a
hundred twenty seconds.

639
00:45:24.635 --> 00:45:29.047
Then log in to the new Grid Master and
check every member has reconnected.

640
00:45:29.047 --> 00:45:34.250
Afterward, wait up to two hours before
any further promotion, and update anything

641
00:45:34.250 --> 00:45:38.986
that points at the old Grid Master's
address, including HSM registrations.

642
00:45:38.986 --> 00:45:43.373
Written down, those steps are the
STIG's documented promotion procedure.

643
00:45:43.373 --> 00:45:51.019
Sources: Infoblox NIOS Backing up
and restoring configuration files;

644
00:45:51.019 --> 00:46:00.117
Restoring backups; Database snapshots; NIOS
9.1.x Promoting a Grid Master Candidate;

645
00:46:00.117 --> 00:46:08.510
DISA Infoblox NIOS 9.x STIG V1R1, V-285271
and V-285273 (mapping is inference).

646
00:46:09.260 --> 00:46:11.610
Logging, and the evidence it produces.

647
00:46:11.610 --> 00:46:14.730
Syslog: in the Grid's
monitoring properties,

648
00:46:14.730 --> 00:46:18.978
log to external syslog servers
over Secure TCP, on port 6514,

649
00:46:18.978 --> 00:46:24.785
with the server's certificate uploaded,
and send it out the management interface.

650
00:46:24.785 --> 00:46:27.425
The audit log: use the Detailed type,

651
00:46:27.425 --> 00:46:30.885
or WAPI Detailed, which
also records API calls.

652
00:46:30.885 --> 00:46:33.739
And turn on Copy Audit
Log Message to Syslog,

653
00:46:33.739 --> 00:46:37.385
which the STIG requires in
addition to external syslog.

654
00:46:37.385 --> 00:46:42.182
DNS queries and responses: Infoblox
warns that turning on the queries and

655
00:46:42.182 --> 00:46:47.373
responses logging categories will
significantly affect system performance.

656
00:46:47.373 --> 00:46:50.251
Use the capture feature or dnstap instead,

657
00:46:50.251 --> 00:46:53.623
and note dnstap drops data
if its receiver is down.

658
00:46:53.623 --> 00:46:56.688
DHCP lease history is
archived and exportable,

659
00:46:56.688 --> 00:47:00.853
and it's the source the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 08

660
00:47:00.853 --> 00:47:04.861
Chapter 11 needs to tie an address
to a device at a point in time.

661
00:47:04.861 --> 00:47:09.474
The Data Connector forwards DNS data
to a SIEM in CEF or LEEF format,

662
00:47:09.474 --> 00:47:12.378
but it's managed from the Infoblox Portal,

663
00:47:12.378 --> 00:47:16.223
so by my inference it isn't
available on a FIPS-mode Grid.

664
00:47:16.223 --> 00:47:19.923
There, use Secure TCP syslog or dnstap.

665
00:47:19.923 --> 00:47:22.148
And the SIEM is the system of record.

666
00:47:22.148 --> 00:47:27.526
OMB memorandum M-26-14 requires
logs searchable for six months and

667
00:47:27.526 --> 00:47:32.611
retrievable for twelve, which
NIOS's local retention can't meet.

668
00:47:32.611 --> 00:47:39.482
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x
Logging to external syslog servers;

669
00:47:39.482 --> 00:47:45.164
Audit log types and rolling;
DNS logging categories;

670
00:47:45.164 --> 00:47:50.054
Capturing DNS queries
and responses; dnstap;

671
00:47:50.054 --> 00:47:58.247
Data Connector overview and registration;
DISA Infoblox NIOS 9.x STIG V1R1,

672
00:47:58.247 --> 00:48:05.090
V-285237; earlier federal brief
(OMB M-26-14); Book 08, Chapter 11.

673
00:48:05.840 --> 00:48:07.840
Automation, and its limits.

674
00:48:07.840 --> 00:48:14.069
Terraform's NIOS provider,
version 2.1.0 from July 2026,

675
00:48:14.069 --> 00:48:19.165
needs NIOS 9.1.0 or later
and WAPI version 2.14.

676
00:48:19.165 --> 00:48:26.215
That settles a question the earlier
research left open: 9.1.0 is WAPI 2.14.

677
00:48:26.215 --> 00:48:33.077
The older 1.x provider works
with 9.0.6 and WAPI 2.13.6.

678
00:48:33.077 --> 00:48:39.038
Version 2 identifies resources by UUID,
and its resources cover Grid members,

679
00:48:39.038 --> 00:48:44.626
upgrade groups and schedules, zones,
views, records, networks, DHCP ranges,

680
00:48:44.626 --> 00:48:48.817
failover, fixed addresses,
managed Microsoft servers,

681
00:48:48.817 --> 00:48:52.728
admin groups and roles,
SAML, and syslog endpoints.

682
00:48:52.728 --> 00:48:55.976
There's also a new combined provider,

683
00:48:55.976 --> 00:49:02.365
version 3.0.0 beta from October 2026,
for NIOS and Universal DDI together.

684
00:49:02.365 --> 00:49:06.640
It isn't backward compatible, so
moving to it means re-importing state.

685
00:49:06.640 --> 00:49:10.110
My inference: build on the
NIOS version 2 provider now,

686
00:49:10.110 --> 00:49:14.290
and plan a state re-import when
version 3 is generally available.

687
00:49:14.290 --> 00:49:19.227
Ansible's NIOS collection, version 1.10.0,

688
00:49:19.227 --> 00:49:24.015
needs WAPI 2.12.3 or
later and Python 3.10.

689
00:49:24.015 --> 00:49:28.683
It covers zones, records,
networks, ranges, members and more,

690
00:49:28.683 --> 00:49:33.253
but it has no module for
failover associations or DNSSEC.

691
00:49:33.253 --> 00:49:34.878
And some things stay manual.

692
00:49:34.878 --> 00:49:38.242
FIPS and Common Criteria
mode, the factory reset,

693
00:49:38.242 --> 00:49:43.203
promoting the candidate, and temporary
licenses are command-line only.

694
00:49:43.203 --> 00:49:45.850
HSM groups and DNSSEC signing have no

695
00:49:45.850 --> 00:49:49.352
Terraform or Ansible
resource in those listings.

696
00:49:49.352 --> 00:49:52.664
Write those as runbooks that
capture their evidence as they go.

697
00:49:52.664 --> 00:50:01.695
Sources: GitHub infobloxopen/terraform-provider-nios
README compatibility matrix and

698
00:50:01.695 --> 00:50:09.504
resource listing (v2.1.0);
infobloxopen/terraform-provider-infoblox releases

699
00:50:09.504 --> 00:50:13.775
(v3.0.0-beta);
infobloxopen/infoblox-ansible

700
00:50:13.775 --> 00:50:18.045
README and modules
(v1.10.0); all accessed Oct.

701
00:50:18.045 --> 00:50:21.690
23, 2026; research brief section 9.

702
00:50:22.440 --> 00:50:25.427
Here's the whole build
mapped onto the Federal

703
00:50:25.427 --> 00:50:28.415
Modernization Workbook's
Book 08's six phases.

704
00:50:28.415 --> 00:50:32.686
This is the program's design guidance:
the order is a recommendation,

705
00:50:32.686 --> 00:50:35.965
and each step rests on the
facts from earlier slides.

706
00:50:35.965 --> 00:50:40.654
Phase 0, decide: FIPS mode,
the long-term-support train,

707
00:50:40.654 --> 00:50:43.490
the DNSSEC defaults, and the HSM.

708
00:50:43.490 --> 00:50:48.115
Phase 1, inventory and baseline:
build a lab Grid first.

709
00:50:48.115 --> 00:50:53.046
Then production in order: the Grid Master
pair with NTP set at the Grid level,

710
00:50:53.046 --> 00:50:57.977
the candidate with a promote test,
permanent licenses, PIV and SAML sign-in

711
00:50:57.977 --> 00:51:02.828
with the banner and the management
port, syslog, and scheduled SCP backups.

712
00:51:02.828 --> 00:51:06.613
Then physical managing members
next to the domain controllers,

713
00:51:06.613 --> 00:51:09.041
with the Windows servers added read-only.

714
00:51:09.041 --> 00:51:15.081
Phase 2, coexist: DNS and DHCP members
on-premises, and vNIOS in each cloud hub,

715
00:51:15.081 --> 00:51:19.952
with high availability only
where the region is documented.

716
00:51:19.952 --> 00:51:23.740
Recreate the forwarders,
and load AES keytabs.

717
00:51:23.740 --> 00:51:29.802
Phase 3, migrate authority: import each
zone, validate, and flip delegation.

718
00:51:29.802 --> 00:51:33.902
Re-sign signed zones on NIOS
and publish the new DS.

719
00:51:33.902 --> 00:51:39.341
For DHCP, scope by scope: lower lease
times, take the scope over, create the

720
00:51:39.341 --> 00:51:43.397
failover association, confirm
the DNS server option,

721
00:51:43.397 --> 00:51:45.978
and deactivate the Windows scope.

722
00:51:45.978 --> 00:51:52.166
Phase 4, federate the clouds, with
Universal DDI only on non-FIPS scopes.

723
00:51:52.166 --> 00:51:58.477
Phase 5, decommission, behind Book 08's
five gates, with the locator zones last.

724
00:51:58.477 --> 00:52:01.702
Disable sync, remove the managed servers,

725
00:52:01.702 --> 00:52:06.253
archive the exports, and
de-authorize the old DHCP servers.

726
00:52:06.253 --> 00:52:11.005
And Phase 6: the remaining STIG items,
the approved long-term-support build

727
00:52:11.005 --> 00:52:15.602
through a test upgrade, and the evidence
checklist in the backup slides.

728
00:52:15.602 --> 00:52:20.954
Sources: Research brief section 10
(phase-by-phase build sequence,

729
00:52:20.954 --> 00:52:26.504
program design guidance); Book 08,
Chapter 10 (six phases, Phase 5 gates,

730
00:52:26.504 --> 00:52:32.510
cutover loop); Infoblox NIOS and DISA
STIG sources cited in Parts 1 to 3.

731
00:52:33.260 --> 00:52:37.235
To close: decisions, vendor
asks, and next steps.

732
00:52:37.235 --> 00:52:38.710
The decisions to take.

733
00:52:38.710 --> 00:52:42.022
FIPS mode, knowing it
costs cloud management.

734
00:52:42.022 --> 00:52:46.834
A hardware security module, or a stealth
Grid Master with a documented exception.

735
00:52:46.834 --> 00:52:50.598
NSEC or NSEC3 for internal zones.

736
00:52:50.598 --> 00:52:53.611
Whether the locator zones stay on AD DNS,

737
00:52:53.611 --> 00:52:59.547
which is the Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 08's default, or move to NIOS.

738
00:52:59.547 --> 00:53:02.847
And how many physical managing
members you need, and where.

739
00:53:02.847 --> 00:53:07.520
Ask Infoblox, in writing: which
build is the current approved federal

740
00:53:07.520 --> 00:53:13.176
long-term-support release; when the FIPS
140-3 and Common Criteria certificates are

741
00:53:13.176 --> 00:53:18.833
expected; whether Kerberos-signed updates
are supported with Windows Server 2025

742
00:53:18.833 --> 00:53:24.407
domain controllers; whether vNIOS high
availability works in GovCloud and Azure

743
00:53:24.407 --> 00:53:29.080
Government; whether clients keep
their addresses in a DHCP takeover;

744
00:53:29.080 --> 00:53:31.785
and whether a SHA-2
NTP key type is coming.

745
00:53:31.785 --> 00:53:32.860
Next steps.

746
00:53:32.860 --> 00:53:37.010
Stand up a lab Grid on
Infoblox's evaluation images.

747
00:53:37.010 --> 00:53:41.322
Set the DNSSEC defaults, then
sign a test zone and validate it.

748
00:53:41.322 --> 00:53:44.822
Pilot one DHCP scope takeover end to end.

749
00:53:44.822 --> 00:53:47.372
And run the STIG
checklist against the lab.

750
00:53:47.372 --> 00:53:52.244
The program's view is plain: Infoblox
NIOS can carry Book 08's design,

751
00:53:52.244 --> 00:53:57.647
and its risks are known and manageable
if the Phase 0 decisions come first.

752
00:53:57.647 --> 00:54:00.559
The backup slides list
the assessor evidence,

753
00:54:00.559 --> 00:54:03.010
the risks, and what couldn't be verified.

754
00:54:03.010 --> 00:54:06.050
This is the program's
research, under review,

755
00:54:06.050 --> 00:54:08.922
not an endorsement by any federal agency.

756
00:54:08.922 --> 00:54:09.685
Thank you.

757
